@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex overview

Dials shows how busy a GNU/Linux system is, live, in a full-screen
terminal interface in the style of the classic two-panel file
managers.  Each subsystem has its own page: an overview, the
processors, memory, graphics processors, disks, network, processes,
hardware sensors and power.

Dials reads its measurements from @file{/proc} and @file{/sys}.  It
keeps the files it reads open and rereads them in place, so it uses
very little of the processor time it measures.

This version is an early skeleton.  The Overview page works; the
other pages describe what they will show.

@node Invoking dials
@chapter Invoking @command{dials}
@cindex invoking
@cindex options

@example
dials [@var{option}]@dots{}
@end example

@table @option
@item -d
@itemx --demo
@opindex --demo
Show simulated data instead of this system's.  This is useful for
trying the interface and for screenshots.

@item -i @var{seconds}
@itemx --interval=@var{seconds}
@opindex --interval
Take a sample every @var{seconds}, which may be a fraction from 0.1 to
3600.  The default is 1.

@item -p @var{name}
@itemx --page=@var{name}
@opindex --page
Start on page @var{name}: one of @samp{overview}, @samp{cpu},
@samp{memory}, @samp{gpu}, @samp{disk}, @samp{network},
@samp{processes}, @samp{sensors} or @samp{power}.

@item -t @var{name}
@itemx --theme=@var{name}
@opindex --theme
Use the color theme @var{name}: @samp{classic} (white on dark navy, the
default), @samp{blue} (white on the terminal's blue), @samp{dark} or
@samp{mono}.  Terminals without color always use @samp{mono}.

@item --once
@opindex --once
Print one plain-text report on standard output and exit, without
starting the full-screen interface.

@item --help
@opindex --help
Print a summary of the options and exit.

@item --version
@opindex --version
Print the version number and licensing information and exit.
@end table

@node The screen
@chapter The screen
@cindex screen layout

The top line is the menu bar.  Press @kbd{F9} to open it; the right
end shows the current page, whether sampling is live, paused or
simulated, the sampling interval and the time.

The bottom line lists what the function keys do.

Between them is the current page.  Graphs scroll from right to left,
with the newest sample on the right; the label on the bottom of a
graph tells how much time it spans.  Bars and graphs are green below
60% of their limit, yellow below 85% and red above.

@node Keys
@chapter Keys
@cindex keys

@table @kbd
@item F1
@itemx h
@itemx ?
Show the list of keys.
@item F2
@itemx p
@itemx @key{SPC}
Pause or resume sampling.
@item F3
@itemx -
Sample less often.
@item F4
@itemx +
Sample more often.
@item F7
@itemx r
Clear the history graphs.
@item F9
Open the menu bar.  Use the arrow keys to move, @key{RET} to choose and
@key{ESC} to close it.
@item F10
@itemx q
Quit.
@item 1 @dots{} 9
@itemx M-1 @dots{} M-9
Go to a page.
@item C-@key{PgUp}
@itemx C-@key{PgDn}
Go to the previous or next page.
@item t
Switch to the next color theme.
@end table

@node Reporting bugs
@chapter Reporting bugs
@cindex bugs, reporting

Please include the output of @samp{dials --version} and
@samp{dials --once} in a bug report, together with the name of the
terminal you use.

